WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.520
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Geoff Duncan, who's the Republican lieutenant

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:10.749
governor of Georgia and also author of the
new book GOP 2.0 How the 2020 Election can

00:00:10.749 --> 00:00:16.660
Lead a Better Way Forward for America's Conservative
Party. It's great to have you on. I appreciate

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.029
it.
Speaker 2: Yeah, thanks for the opportunity.

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:24.689
Speaker 1: So, OK, I mean, where to start,
right? Georgia has been at the center of significant

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:29.919
political controversy. We now know about the
various phone calls and requests to put it

00:00:29.919 --> 00:00:34.910
lightly that were made by former President
Trump when it comes to the results of the

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:43.820
2020 Georgia election. I mean, is it? Give
us your sense of the direction that the Republican

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:49.480
Party has gone relative to where you would
like to see it as a conservative party?

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:54.100
Speaker 2: Yeah, there's definitely chaos
on the battlefield, for sure. And certainly

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.730
we're watching that play out in Georgia, and
I think Georgia is just a microcosm of what's

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:03.490
what's playing out all over the country. You
know, I'm one of those people that I'm hardwired

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:07.620
to be a conservative. I grew up that way.
I went to college that way. I started my first

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.831
business, raise my family. I just believe
in the conservative principles. And so it

00:01:11.831 --> 00:01:19.210
pains me to watch the inability to win elections
because of a stylistic, because of personalities,

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:23.490
not because of our policies. That's painful
to watch. And then that's kind of the process

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.200
and the tug of war that we're in right now
as a Republican Party, that's really what

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.101
drove me to write GOP 2.0. It's not really
any sort of watered down, you know, policy

00:01:33.101 --> 00:01:37.000
positions. As a conservative, it's just about
a better approach to deliver those policy

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.360
positions to America.
Speaker 1: So did did you vote for Donald

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.780
Trump in 2020?
Speaker 2: I did, I did, you know, I voted

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.520
for him in 2016, I voted for him in 2020.
I probably put myself in that category of

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.090
tens of millions of Republicans that did the
same thing that, you know, really, really

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:59.880
applauded the conservative wins over those
four years. But it's hard to explain to even

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.890
my own kids. I talk about this in the book
quite a bit like my three boys really shaped

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:09.950
a lot of kind of my positions just in raising
them with my wife. It was hard to explain,

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:13.779
you know, the tweets that went out, you know,
I spent all this time telling my kids, you

00:02:13.779 --> 00:02:18.680
know, Hey, be careful what you do on social
media, you know, and then how do I explain

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.430
the former president doing what he did? Well,
how did you

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:25.610
Speaker 1: how did you explain? How did you
explain voting for him then?

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:30.389
Speaker 2: Yeah, so interesting. I talk a
lot about it and story, you know, mine was

00:02:30.389 --> 00:02:37.870
I. I compared the Biden ideas and plans forward.
Really, what I explained it, or at least rationalized

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:42.579
in my mind, is really what's playing out right
now with Joe Biden. You know, I mean, nobody,

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:47.830
this is a nonpartisan answer to this. Nobody
agrees in my my mind that the Afghanistan

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:52.349
handoff was the way it was should have been
handled the way we're dealing with inflation.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:56.529
The southern border, the only person I mean,
everybody believes in border security, except

00:02:56.529 --> 00:03:01.090
for Joe Biden. So I put those elements against
what Donald Trump had done as a conservative

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:05.930
and and I pulled it. I pulled the the ballot
for President Trump.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:10.290
Speaker 1: What exactly would Trump have done
differently on Afghanistan? Because he was

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.840
actually asked on a Newsmax interview, he
couldn't articulate a single thing he would

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.510
have done differently.
Speaker 2: Yeah, I think looking back now

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:23.090
in the hindsight of twenty twenty Monday morning
quarterback looks, he didn't really have an

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:29.150
exit strategy out of there, as thorough as
I probably thought he did. But I hope that

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:32.779
there was enough conservative leadership in
his administration that would have given him

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:39.389
better advice than what Joe Biden did and
how he executed the the exit out of Afghanistan.

00:03:39.389 --> 00:03:42.019
That would be my hope, but it's only it's
only hypothetical.

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:45.969
Speaker 1: Yeah, no, because it seems since
it's the first thing you mentioned that you're

00:03:45.969 --> 00:03:50.590
not pleased with in terms of Biden, but then
Trump can articulate what he would have done

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:55.540
differently. And you sort of can either other
than to say it's a hope you have. It doesn't.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:00.480
It's interesting to me that that's not stylistic.
Like at first you said it, you know that they're

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:07.400
mostly stylistic differences with Trump. But
but policy was where you were. You would like

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.819
to see the policies enacted. But then, like
on Afghanistan, it's not really clear the

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:15.389
policy would have been any different. So I'm
trying to understand sort of whether your

00:04:15.389 --> 00:04:19.160
disagreement is just on style or whether it's
policy as well.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:24.140
Speaker 2: Well, we can split hairs all day
long. There's a president that's in office

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.210
and made the decisions, and his name is Joe
Biden, and there's a former president that's

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:31.930
not in office that did make those decisions
on exiting Afghanistan. The reality is, I

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:35.780
would have hoped that more military minds
would have made those realistic decisions,

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:39.720
and they would have had more timely, thoughtful
consideration for those that help support

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:45.050
our military, those that were fully expecting
to be helped through any sort of military

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:48.821
transition or exit out of the out of the country.
My hope is any president, whether they're

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:52.280
a Democrat or Republican, would have been
more thoughtful for that. But that didn't

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:55.360
happen. And I think that's the same thing
that we're watching play out with the southern

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.449
border. I believe in border security. It's
one of the things I talk a lot about in the

00:04:59.449 --> 00:05:04.020
book. I think Republicans have totally dropped
the ball, including former President Trump

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.530
on immigration. We spend all this energy kind
of fearmongering about the southern border.

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:13.070
Yeah, and we built four hundred and sixty
miles of border wall out of a, what, two thousand

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:19.100
mile border or more. And the reason why I
think we've dropped the ball is we're only

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:24.460
having a singular focus conversation about
a big issue like immigration, border security,

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.939
but the real conversation. In addition, there's
got to be the 60 million undocumented folks

00:05:28.939 --> 00:05:33.160
that are here, many of which we have been
full force, allowing them to be embedded in

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.590
our communities and in our economy. And we
got to have a real conversation about that.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:41.949
That, to me, is an area of opportunity for
Republicans to join ranks with some of the

00:05:41.949 --> 00:05:45.560
folks on the other side of the aisle to have
those real conversations. And I talk a lot

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:49.229
about that in the book.
Speaker 1: I'm curious, this is sort of kind

00:05:49.229 --> 00:05:56.800
of like a it's a sanity check of sorts when
during the 2016 campaign where you did end

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:03.169
up voting for Donald Trump, when he said during
his first term he would build a wall across

00:06:03.169 --> 00:06:09.229
all 2000 miles and Mexico would pay for it.
Did you believe that for a second? I did I'm

00:06:09.229 --> 00:06:14.919
sorry, did you say you did not? I did not.
You did not. OK. Why do you think so many

00:06:14.919 --> 00:06:19.539
people did believe that when he said it, when
it was so obviously going to not happen?

00:06:19.539 --> 00:06:24.009
Speaker 2: Well, I think that's the riddle
that we're trying to solve right now is the

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:28.639
Republican Party is how can we get people
away? And this isn't just a Republican promise,

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:35.330
a Democrat from to, you know, America's addicted
to 10 second soundbites. We're making massive

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:39.960
political decisions and putting our support
behind folks. In short clips and certainly

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:45.430
GOP 2.0 is really trying to build a much more
thoughtful, thorough plan of attack on some

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:50.840
of these big issues ones that aren't about
280 characters on Twitter or about some slogan

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:55.270
called Build the Wall. What really drew the
most ire from the former president to me,

00:06:55.270 --> 00:07:00.539
specifically during the 10 weeks of my very
intense rebuttal of the conspiracy theories

00:07:00.539 --> 00:07:05.310
and the fraudulent, you know, the real fraud
was talking about fraud in the election cycle

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:11.379
here in Georgia. So my my major pushback really
started when I said, Look, Build the Wall

00:07:11.379 --> 00:07:15.930
is a great project name, but it's not an immigration
policy. We, as Republicans, need to grasp

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:22.479
on to a serious immigration policy and start
to move forward. You know, that really drew

00:07:22.479 --> 00:07:28.039
the ire of the former president, really started
a a visceral downhill spiral on whatever relationship

00:07:28.039 --> 00:07:31.629
that was out there. Yeah.
Speaker 1: So like what happened during those

00:07:31.629 --> 00:07:39.669
days between Election Day 2020, where we ultimately
learned the margin of victory for Joe Biden

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:45.319
in Georgia? Subsequently, the phone calls,
I need Brad Raffensperger to find me eleven

00:07:45.319 --> 00:07:49.580
thousand seven hundred and eighty votes give
me a break. Like, what was what were you doing

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:53.130
at that time? Who were you talking to? What
were the conversations like?

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:58.810
Speaker 2: Yeah, so I literally within days
of the election and all of this kind of rumor

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:03.400
mill starting that there was fraud and whatnot,
I I don't headfirst into the details. I talked

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.921
to the governor's office. I talked to the
secretary of state and his entire team. I

00:08:06.921 --> 00:08:10.890
talked to the attorney general. And really
the feedback I was getting is, Jeff, there's

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:15.539
there's no signs of any sort of systemic fraud.
There's nothing coming in other than just

00:08:15.539 --> 00:08:20.349
wildly placed social media posts and wild
allegations starting to flow in from the Trump

00:08:20.349 --> 00:08:25.660
administration. So I started to get as quickly
as I could get on TV for for two reasons.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:30.349
One is I didn't want any sort of runaway rumor
to take over the control of the narrative

00:08:30.349 --> 00:08:35.790
that we can run a fair, legal, you know, legal
election year in Georgia. And to what I was

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:39.620
trying to protect the runoff process from
two conservative U.S. senators that were now

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:45.450
engaged in a runoff. Unfortunately, we know
how that turned out if you're a conservative.

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:50.070
So I hit the TV, I probably did. I don't know
what the number is 60, 70, 80, 100 national

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:56.220
television interviews and radio and music
or print interviews, and I spoke up against

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:59.320
it. I was one of the first folks that said,
Unfortunately, I'm going to have to say Joe

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:06.990
Biden is the president elect. And almost instantly
drew ire from not only the former president,

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:13.020
but also from, you know, people all over,
all over the country. We end up, you know,

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:17.150
receiving threats. As a family. We have 24
hour security. I mean, it was it was an intense

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:23.320
10 weeks of simply, you know, just telling
the truth. And one of the surprising things

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:29.850
for me was, and one of the motivating factors
behind writing the book GOP 2.0 was, you know,

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:34.670
I thought I was walking out singing as part
of a Republican choir. But the reality was

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:41.060
I was singing a solo for far too long. When
the facts simply lined up that there was no

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:46.200
fraud, there was no systemic issues going
on. Donald Trump got beat, and he's the one

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:50.350
who missed the layup. And as a Republican,
I hold him accountable for not doing the things

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:55.201
he needed to do to win.
Speaker 1: Was there behind the scenes? Any

00:09:55.201 --> 00:10:02.230
consideration among Brian Kemp, Brad Raffensperger
and other election officials of actually trying

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:05.200
to do what Donald Trump was asking to have
done?

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:10.031
Speaker 2: So I only speak for myself, and
the answer is no, there was no way I was going

00:10:10.031 --> 00:10:15.800
to move my feet. This job is not worth lying
for. And I certainly wouldn't lie to 11 million

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:21.340
Georgians, some of them who who wanted to
see this scenario changed. But I certainly

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:26.120
never heard of any time. I never had a conversation
with any of the other statewide elected officials

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:31.180
that ever appeared to me that they were considering
it, but I'd encourage you to get their opinion

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:34.320
on that.
Speaker 1: Sure. So I guess as opinions aside,

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.901
as far as you know,
Speaker 2: no, just as far as I know, there

00:10:37.901 --> 00:10:42.980
was never a consideration between the governor
or the speaker, the secretary of state and

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:47.840
myself to to take into consideration because
look, there was multiple avenues going at

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:52.710
this point. There were, you know, the former
president wanted to have a special session

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:57.320
to send a different set of electorates. We
wanted to have, you know, just all types of

00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:00.270
different scenarios. We're playing out. Just
really weren't feasible.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:06.290
Speaker 1: Do you think that the craziness
that took place involving these fraud claims

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:10.420
between November and the date of the special
elections for the Senate, which I think was

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:15.760
January 5th? Do you think that contributed
to both Republican senators losing their races?

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:22.440
Speaker 2: Yeah, one hundred percent. Absolutely.
And it was unfortunate. I don't claim to be

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:27.530
smarter than anybody by any stretch of the
imagination. I'm just a retired baseball player,

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:33.390
for the most part. But I could see it coming
from a mile away when they buy just by default

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:38.110
hired Donald Trump as their campaign manager
during the runoffs. That was the beginning

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:44.240
to the end. Georgians wanted to be reminded
of their conservative policies and positions

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:49.550
that they stood for. They know Georgia fell
in love with their business acumen and their

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:55.100
ability to articulate jobs and national security,
and all those things that really kind of are

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:59.670
the tenets of why, you know, we wanted them
there and why the governor and selected Kelly

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:05.720
Loeffler to represent Johnny Isaacson of the
remainder of his term. They forgot to do that.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:10.580
And unfortunately, Donald Trump got his hooks
in them. And, you know, just made it all about

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:16.720
him and his conspiracy theories.
Speaker 1: Do you think that Trump's actions

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:21.380
in terms of the influence campaign and the
phone calls might have been criminal?

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:26.940
Speaker 2: So I'm not an attorney, I told
you I'm a recovering baseball player. Sure,

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:32.440
but based on conversations we have there.
Yeah. So look, I think I think I'll start

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:39.370
with they were absolutely inappropriate and
they were discussing whether I was whatever

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:43.390
party affiliations were associated with it.
They were just inappropriate. And I know that

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:46.950
there's investigations going on and they're
going to certainly get to the bottom of what

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:51.390
their opinions are from those legal investigations.
But I don't have the I don't have the legal

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:55.360
acumen not to split hairs. You know, if it
was illegal or not, it was inappropriate,

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:58.850
for sure.
Speaker 1: Fair. One of the the criticisms

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:04.030
I've had of the Republican Party of late as
someone who's not a Republican is that the

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:10.500
focus on policy has become close to zero and
the most prominent Republicans right now that

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:16.330
that get headlines federally are getting headlines
by saying that they don't want to wear masks

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:21.260
or they don't want people to have to get vaccinated,
or they're taking a stance against critical

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:27.280
race theory or forces trying to cancel Christmas.
Or now it's Halloween, I guess Fox did a bunch

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:32.291
of segments yesterday about Halloween maybe
getting canceled. Do you think that this is

00:13:32.291 --> 00:13:37.450
a direction that's good for just just without
evaluating it morally, for winning races,

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:41.850
for winning elections? Is it good to focus
on the culture stuff instead of policy?

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:46.920
Speaker 2: Absolutely not. I spent a lot of
time in the book talking about it, because

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:50.780
look at the end of the day, we're all supposed
to be running for these offices because we

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:55.550
want we want to be in a position of leadership.
Right. And there's no leadership to be found

00:13:55.550 --> 00:14:00.360
in all these short term sugar high issues.
I talk about in the book what I refer to as

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:06.350
my pet project policy. Empathy a tone really
is a strategy to approve all of those as a

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:10.740
meaningful way to win back the White House
in twenty four. And on the policy front, yeah,

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:14.530
we we have literally forgotten to remind America
of the policies that we, as Republicans, are

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:18.570
good at. Just like you mentioned, you're you're
you're part of the, you know, you're a Democrat.

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:22.040
Speaker 1: I'm not a Democrat. I'm I'm on
the left, but I'm not a Democrat.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:26.130
Speaker 2: OK. OK, so so you're on the left.
So there's certain issues that you think the

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:29.830
left are better at right. That's your personal
position. Sure. Well, I think there's some

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:34.260
things that Americans think that Republicans
are better at. We just have totally forgotten

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:37.810
to remind people of those policies that we're
good at. And we've also forgotten the ability

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:41.080
to understand that we can lead on some of
these big issues. I talked about immigration

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:46.390
a second ago. Let's talk about health care.
Right. It is a it is an issue ripe for the

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:51.840
picking. To talk about something other than
we hate Obamacare more, you know, more to

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:54.810
11, right? You know, we need to put more on
the bone there. We need to talk about how

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:59.710
we center health care away from the government
and onto the small business owners. How do

00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:03.750
we empower small business owners who, by the
way, are the bulk of the employers in this

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:08.380
country to be able to compete with big companies
and with government programs to be able to

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:12.650
offer affordable health insurance to their
employees and to be able to entice people

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:17.090
to come to work instead of just going to the
mailbox to receive those. There's so many

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:19.930
solutions that Republicans can come up with
health care, but we've got to be willing to

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:24.110
talk about it and not these sideshows and
these distraction elements and these shiny

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:28.990
objects that that really kind of just run
to day news cycles. And then we then we got

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:32.700
to hurry up, find the next one.
Speaker 1: Yeah. You mentioned 2024 and taking

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:37.780
back the White House, obviously, before that,
we have a midterm election in 2022. Do you

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:46.330
think that it helps your um, sort of. I don't
know that I call it aside, but your approach

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:52.870
to the Republican Party is it helped or hindered
if folks like Marjorie Taylor Greene and Lauren

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:56.940
Boebert and Madison Cawthorn get reelected
next year.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:02.810
Speaker 2: So I don't I don't know. The only
person I really know in that mix that I know

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:07.820
well is Marjorie Taylor Greene, and certainly
if we have more Marjorie Taylor Greene in

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:13.420
Congress, we're in trouble as Republicans,
right? That's right. And I certainly have

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:18.380
made no no qualms about it that I think she's
destructive to our state. I think she's destructive

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:23.340
to our party. I think she's a person like
hers, destructive to our country. And so it's

